<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
	Required-reason API declarations. Sustain's own code only touches one
	category directly: UserDefaults, via @AppStorage (weightUnitRaw,
	weeklySummaryText/GeneratedAt, ReminderSettingsView's protein check-in
	prefs) and EntitlementManager's isPremium/hasMaintenanceProgram flags.
	Reason CA92.1 ("access info from the same app") is correct here — there's
	no App Group, App Clip, or extension sharing these defaults with anything
	else. No FileManager, disk-space, or system-uptime APIs are called
	directly anywhere in the app. If a future SDK (Supabase, RevenueCat,
	TelemetryDeck) is added, check whether it ships its own privacy manifest
	or requires additional entries here — third-party SDKs are a common
	source of manifest omissions that get caught at review, not before.

	[EXTENDED 2026-09] `PatternNoticeStore.swift` (the "Pip Notices" feature —
	see `Services/PatternDetectionEngine.swift`) added two more UserDefaults
	keys (seen pattern instances, muted pattern types). Still covered by this
	same CA92.1 declaration, not a new entry: same category (UserDefaults),
	same reason (same-app access only), no new API surface. Worth calling out
	explicitly anyway, per this comment's own standing instruction above, so
	a future reader confirms that rather than assuming it. This feature does
	not otherwise touch anything requiring a declaration here — the pattern
	detection itself is pure on-device arithmetic over data already logged,
	sends nothing anywhere, and the resulting notification is a plain local
	`UNUserNotificationCenter` request, already covered by the same
	notification permission every existing reminder in this app already uses.
	-->
	<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes</key>
	<array>
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryUserDefaults</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
			<array>
				<string>CA92.1</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>

	<!--
	Sustain does not track users across apps or websites, and never calls
	ATTrackingManager (no NSUserTrackingUsageDescription in Info.plist either —
	that key was removed since the app never actually requests tracking
	authorization; keeping an unused ATT prompt string was flagged by App
	Store Connect as inconsistent with these "no tracking" answers at
	submission time). No tracking domains, because there is no tracking.
	-->
	<key>NSPrivacyTracking</key>
	<false/>
	<key>NSPrivacyTrackingDomains</key>
	<array/>

	<!--
	Mirrors what must also be entered in App Store Connect's privacy
	questionnaire — this manifest doesn't replace that, it documents it.

	[CORRECTED 2026-07-30 — this comment previously said "never leaves the
	device today (no backend exists yet)," which stopped being true the
	moment the Supabase Edge Function AI proxy shipped and was never updated
	here, even though the in-app Settings copy and the published privacy
	policy both were (see CHANGELOG.md's Guideline 1.4.1/3.1.2(c) fix entry).
	A stale privacy-manifest comment is low-risk on its own — reviewers look
	at the declared data types below and the App Store Connect questionnaire,
	not code comments — but leaving an incorrect "nothing leaves the device"
	claim sitting in the one file whose entire job is describing data
	practices was exactly the kind of drift that caused this app's only two
	real rejections (1.4.1, 3.1.2(c)): documentation quietly falling out of
	sync with what the shipped app actually does. Fixed here so it can't be
	the source of a future Guideline 5.1.1 mismatch (declared privacy
	practices vs. observed behavior) if anyone edits this file by reading
	its own comments instead of the current code.]

	[CORRECTED AGAIN, 2026-08-01, caught in a pre-2.0-submission audit — this
	is exactly the "revisit this" trigger the paragraph below used to warn
	about, and it wasn't revisited when it should have been: Sign in with
	Apple + Supabase account sync shipped in the 2026-07-30 session (see
	CHANGELOG.md's "Sign in with Apple + Supabase account sync" entry and
	APP_REVIEW_NOTES.md §7-8), and this file was never updated for it. Two
	real gaps existed until now: (1) the paragraph below still claimed health
	data "never leaves the device through any Sustain-operated storage,"
	which stopped being true the moment sync shipped — Supabase IS
	Sustain-operated storage, and every log type now syncs there by design,
	for every user, not as an opt-in extra. (2) Signing in collects an email
	address (real or Apple's private-relay address) and creates an account
	identifier (a Supabase Auth user ID) — neither was declared as a
	collected data type at all. Both fixed below: the Health entry's purpose
	list now includes account-sync functionality explicitly in this comment,
	and two new dict entries (Email Address, User ID) cover the sign-in data
	itself. APP_REVIEW_NOTES.md §7 already flagged that the App Store Connect
	privacy questionnaire needs the same update — this file alone does not
	satisfy that; both need to match.]

	Health data (weight, dietary protein, dose/side-effect logs, medication
	plan) is linked to the user's own account and now genuinely leaves the
	device for two reasons: account sync (Supabase, so it's available again
	after a reinstall or on a second device — required for every signed-in
	user, not opt-in, since Sign in with Apple is mandatory app-wide) and, for
	two specific opt-in features, forwarding to a third-party AI provider
	(Anthropic, via a stateless Supabase Edge Function proxy that forwards the
	request and stores nothing itself) — a user-chosen food photo for the AI
	protein estimate, and a small set of already-aggregated weekly figures
	(not raw logs) for the weekly AI recap. Only the AI features are
	user-initiated per use; account sync runs automatically for every
	signed-in user as core app functionality (that's what "required" means
	for the Health entry's purpose below), not a separate opt-in toggle. See
	the Photos/Videos entry below for the food-photo case specifically.
	Email Address and User ID are new entries below for the Sign in with
	Apple + Supabase account itself — both required, since account sync
	fundamentally requires an identity to sync to.
	-->
	<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes</key>
	<array>
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeHealth</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<true/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<!--
		[UPDATED 2026-08-03 — two new photo-using features shipped since this
		entry was written, and it was reviewed against both rather than
		assumed still correct.]

		This entry stays exactly as it was, and here is why, per feature:

		1. AI protein estimate (unchanged, the original reason for this
		   entry): a user-chosen food photo is transmitted off-device to the
		   Anthropic proxy for processing and is not stored by Sustain. That
		   is "collected" under Apple's definition, hence this entry.
		2. Lab report import (new): same shape as #1 — a user-chosen photo of
		   a lab report is transmitted to the same proxy for transcription,
		   never stored by Sustain (only the transcribed numbers the user
		   confirms are saved, as text, locally). Already covered by this
		   entry; no new dict needed, since it's the same data type, the same
		   linkage, and the same App Functionality purpose.
		3. Progress photos (new): these are stored ON DEVICE ONLY, in the
		   app's own SwiftData store, and are deliberately NOT synced to
		   Supabase and never transmitted anywhere (see
		   `ProgressPhotosView`'s header comment for that decision). Data
		   that never leaves the device is not "collected" under Apple's
		   definition, so this feature adds no new declaration here. If
		   progress-photo sync is ever built, this entry must be revisited:
		   photos would then become linked, stored, Sustain-operated data,
		   which is a materially different declaration than the transient
		   processing above.
		-->
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePhotosorVideos</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<!--
		Added 2026-08-01 alongside the sign-in/sync privacy pass above.
		Sign in with Apple can supply either the user's real email or an
		Apple-generated private-relay address (Sustain never sees which);
		either way it's collected and linked to the account for
		authentication and sync, not for tracking or advertising.


		[UPDATED 2026-08-01, same day, once "Continue with Google" shipped
		alongside Apple.] This entry already covers Google too - it's the same
		data category (an email address, linked to the account, for
		authentication/sync) regardless of which provider supplies it, so no
		new dict was needed here. Two things worth confirming, not because
		either changed anything in this file, but because they're exactly the
		kind of provider-specific detail that could get missed:
		(1) GoogleSignIn-iOS ships its own bundled privacy manifest inside its
		xcframework; Xcode aggregates it into the app's final privacy report
		automatically, so it needs no manual entries here.
		(2) Google's SDK can also return a display name and profile photo URL
		on the `GIDGoogleUser` it hands back (`result.user.profile?.name`/
		`imageURL`) - `AuthManager.completeGoogleSignIn`/`linkGoogleIdentity`
		deliberately never reads either, only `idToken`, `accessToken`, and
		`profile?.email`, so no Name or Photo data type entry is needed. If a
		future change starts reading either of those two fields, this file and
		the App Store Connect questionnaire both need a new entry before that
		ships, not after.
		-->
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeEmailAddress</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<true/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<!--
		The Supabase Auth user ID (a UUID) every synced record is keyed to —
		this is the actual mechanism Row Level Security uses to keep one
		person's data isolated from everyone else's (see
		supabase/schema/account_sync.sql). Required for the app to function
		at all once signed in, not optional or tracking-related.
		-->
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeUserID</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<true/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<!--
		Found in a pre-2.2-submission audit, exactly the kind of drift this
		file's own top comment warns about ("if a future SDK is added, check
		whether it ... requires additional entries here"). `AIFeatureEngagementTracker.swift`
		(added earlier this session, see its own header comment) pushes
		per-AI-feature usage counts and last-used timestamps to RevenueCat as
		subscriber attributes — deliberately not a new third-party SDK
		(RevenueCat is already integrated for billing and already covered by
		the privacy policy), but it IS a new category of data leaving the
		device: which AI features a subscriber uses and when, not just
		purchase/billing state. This was never declared here or in the privacy
		policy until this pass. Linked (tied to the subscriber's RevenueCat/
		account identity, which is how a retention cohort works at all) but
		explicitly not tracking (never shared with a data broker or used for
		cross-app/cross-site ad targeting — see `AIFeatureEngagementTracker`'s
		own doc comment for the full "why RevenueCat, not a new SDK" reasoning).
		`Documentation/privacy-policy.md` and the App Store Connect privacy
		questionnaire both need the matching update — this file alone
		documents the practice, it doesn't populate either of those.

		[EXTENDED 2026-08-12] This same entry now also covers a second,
		separate data flow: `AnalyticsManager.swift`'s TelemetryDeck
		integration (see that file's header comment for the full reasoning —
		built to close PROJECT_HANDOFF.md's long-flagged "TelemetryDeck or
		any analytics — still not started" gap). It's declared as the same
		data type rather than a second dict because it has the identical
		shape: linked (correlated to the account ID, not anonymous-forever —
		see AnalyticsManager's "why the account ID, not a fresh UUID"
		reasoning), not tracking (no ad identifiers, no cross-app/cross-site
		sharing, no data broker), same two purposes (App Functionality +
		Analytics). What's new in *this* flow specifically: onboarding-step
		reach/completion, app-open events (for retention), paywall-view
		events (which screen/feature triggered it), and AI-feature
		attempt/completion signals — screen and feature *names* only, never
		message content, photos, health values, or any logged data itself.
		A genuinely different vendor from RevenueCat above, so
		`Documentation/privacy-policy.md`'s "Analytics" paragraph names both
		by name rather than treating this as covered by the existing
		RevenueCat-usage-analytics language.
		-->
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeProductInteraction</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<true/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAnalytics</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
